@page "/platform/users"
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Platform.Services
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Platform.ViewModels
@inherits MvvmComponentBase<PlatformUsersViewModel>

@*
    The vendor's own operator accounts, beside the clinics they administer.

    Every account here is a super admin, and the screen says why rather than offering a
    role picker: the vendor's tenant has no patients, no diary and no invoices, so an
    operator who was not a super admin could do nothing at all.

    The prominent warning is about having only one. There is no password reset in this app
    — no email loop, no recovery code — so a second operator is the whole of the recovery
    plan, and that is not something a vendor should discover on the day it matters.
*@

<PageTitle>Platform users — Molargo</PageTitle>

<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1 border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-4">
    <h4 class="m-0">Platform users</h4>

    <div class="text-[13px] text-ink/55">
        Signed in as @ViewModel.ActingAs · vendor account
    </div>
</div>

@*
    Both outcomes land in the same place: directly under the screen header, one at a
    time, with a failure winning. Before this they were two mechanisms — a refusal
    above the header and a success below it — and a refused save left the previous
    success still showing underneath it, which read as though both had happened.
*@
@if (ViewModel.HasError)
{
    <NoticeComponent Kind="NoticeKind.Failure"
                     Headline="@ViewModel.ErrorHeadline"
                     Message="@ViewModel.ErrorMessage" />
}
else if (ViewModel.LastAction is { Length: > 0 } action)
{
    <NoticeComponent Message="@action" />
}

@if (!ViewModel.IsPermitted)
{
    <div class="px-6 py-5">
        <p class="m-0 text-sm text-ink/70">
            This screen belongs to the vendor's own account. Nothing here is available to a
            practice's own administrators, and the data behind it is not either.
        </p>
    </div>
}
else
{
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_1fr]">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-divider px-6 py-5 lg:border-r-2">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
                <h5 class="m-0">
                    Operators
                    <span class="font-normal text-ink/55">
                        — @ViewModel.ActiveCount active
                    </span>
                </h5>

                <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                        disabled="@ViewModel.IsBusy"
                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.NewOperatorCommand.Execute()">
                    Add an operator
                </button>
            </div>

            @if (ViewModel.IsSinglePointOfFailure)
            {
                <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
                     role="note">
                    <strong>One working account.</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
                        There is no password reset in this app — no email loop and no
                        recovery code — so losing this one locks the vendor out of every
                        clinic's subscription with no way back in. Add a second operator.
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @if (ViewModel.Operators.Count == 0)
            {
                <p class="m-0 text-sm text-ink/55">No operator accounts.</p>
            }
            else
            {
                <div class="flex flex-col">
                    @foreach (var person in ViewModel.Operators)
                    {
                        <div class="@RowCss(person.IsActive, ViewModel.IsOpenFor(person.ProviderId))">
                            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
                                <strong class="text-[13px]">@person.Name</strong>

                                @if (person.IsSelf)
                                {
                                    <span class="ml-2 tag tag-neutral">You</span>
                                }

                                @if (!person.IsActive)
                                {
                                    <span class="ml-2 tag tag-neutral">Deactivated</span>
                                }
                                else if (person.IsLockedOut)
                                {
                                    <span class="ml-2 tag tag-accent">Locked out</span>
                                }
                                else if (person.NeedsPassword)
                                {
                                    <span class="ml-2 tag tag-accent">No password</span>
                                }

                                <span class="block text-xs text-ink/55">
                                    <span class="font-mono">@(person.Username ?? "no username")</span>
                                    @if (person.Email is { Length: > 0 } email)
                                    {
                                        <text> · @email</text>
                                    }
                                </span>

                                <span class="block text-xs text-ink/55">
                                    @if (person.LastSignInUtc is { } seen)
                                    {
                                        <text>
                                            Last signed in
                                            @seen.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM yyyy, h:mm tt")
                                        </text>
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <text>Has never signed in</text>
                                    }
                                </span>

                                @if (person.IsLockedOut && person.LockedUntilUtc is { } until)
                                {
                                    <span class="block text-xs text-accent-700">
                                        Locked until @until.ToLocalTime().ToString("h:mm tt")
                                        after too many failed attempts.
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>

                            <div class="flex shrink-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-1.5">
                                @*
                                    Not disabled while busy, unlike the buttons that
                                    write. These only open a panel, and disabling them for
                                    the length of the initial load is precisely what made
                                    an early click appear to do nothing.
                                *@
                                <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectOperatorCommand.ExecuteAsync(person.ProviderId)">
                                    Edit
                                </button>

                                <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                        @onclick="() => ViewModel.StartResetCommand.ExecuteAsync(person.ProviderId)">
                                    Set password
                                </button>

                                @if (person.IsLockedOut)
                                {
                                    <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                            disabled="@ViewModel.IsBusy"
                                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.UnlockCommand.ExecuteAsync(person.ProviderId)">
                                        Unlock
                                    </button>
                                }

                                @*
                                    No deactivate button on your own row at all. The
                                    service refuses it too — this is what stops the press
                                    rather than explaining it afterwards.
                                *@
                                @if (person.IsSelf)
                                {
                                    <span class="text-xs text-ink/45">
                                        Another operator deactivates you
                                    </span>
                                }
                                else if (person.IsActive)
                                {
                                    <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap text-accent-700"
                                            disabled="@ViewModel.IsBusy"
                                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.DeactivateCommand.ExecuteAsync(person.ProviderId)">
                                        Deactivate
                                    </button>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <button class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                            disabled="@ViewModel.IsBusy"
                                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.ReactivateCommand.ExecuteAsync(person.ProviderId)">
                                        Reactivate
                                    </button>
                                }
                            </div>

                        </div>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            <p class="m-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
                Every account here is a super admin: it can add clinics, edit their
                subscriptions and suspend them. Deactivating an operator never deletes
                them, so their audit entries stay attributed to the person who made them.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 px-6 py-5">
            @*
                One panel, three states: the edit form, the password form, or the notes.
                Setting a password used to be a box wedged into the operator's row while
                this column showed something else entirely — two editors open at once,
                competing for attention. Both now land here, and only one at a time.
            *@
            @if (ViewModel.IsSettingPassword)
            {
                <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                    <h5 class="m-0">Set a password</h5>

                    <button class="btn btn-ghost whitespace-nowrap"
                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.CancelEditCommand.Execute()">
                        Cancel
                    </button>
                </div>

                @*
                    Who it is for, read-only. Shown rather than assumed: this panel is on
                    the opposite side of the screen from the row that opened it, and
                    setting the wrong person's password is not a mistake anybody notices
                    until they cannot sign in.
                *@
                <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                    <strong>@ViewModel.SubjectName</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 flex flex-col gap-1 text-xs">
                        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
                            <span class="text-ink/55">Username</span>
                            <strong class="font-mono">
                                @(ViewModel.SubjectUsername ?? "not set")
                            </strong>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
                            <span class="text-ink/55">Email</span>
                            <strong class="text-right">
                                @(ViewModel.SubjectEmail ?? "not recorded")
                            </strong>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
                            <span class="text-ink/55">Role</span>
                            <strong>Super admin</strong>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-new-password">
                        New password
                    </label>

                    @*
                        Show reveals only what is being typed — the stored password is
                        never rendered into the page, so there is nothing here that could
                        reveal the old one.

                        This comment sits outside the tag on purpose: a Razor comment
                        inside an element's attribute list compiles without complaint and
                        silently drops the element and everything after it.
                    *@
                    <div class="flex gap-2">
                        <input id="op-new-password" class="input w-full"
                               type="@(ViewModel.ShowPassword ? "text" : "password")"
                               autocomplete="new-password"
                               @bind="ViewModel.DraftPassword" @bind:event="oninput" />

                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                @onclick="() => ViewModel.ToggleShowPasswordCommand.Execute()">
                            @(ViewModel.ShowPassword ? "Hide" : "Show")
                        </button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-accent-700">
                        Twelve characters or more. It takes effect immediately and clears
                        any sign-in lockout.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-t border-divider pt-3">
                    <button class="btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap"
                            disabled="@(!ViewModel.CanSetPassword)"
                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.SetPasswordCommand.ExecuteAsync()">
                        Set password
                    </button>

                    <span class="text-xs text-ink/55">
                        Tell them out of band — nothing here emails it.
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
                     role="note">
                    <strong>The old password is not needed.</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
                        This is an administrative reset, and the only recovery this app has
                        — no email loop and no recovery code. Which is also why one operator
                        can never reset their own way back in: it takes a second account.
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
            else if (ViewModel.HasEditor)
            {
                <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                    <h5 class="m-0">@ViewModel.EditorTitle</h5>

                    <button class="btn btn-ghost whitespace-nowrap"
                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.CancelEditCommand.Execute()">
                        Cancel
                    </button>
                </div>

                <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                    <div class="min-w-[140px] flex-1">
                        <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-first">
                            First name
                        </label>
                        <input id="op-first" class="input w-full"
                               @bind="ViewModel.FirstName" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    </div>

                    <div class="min-w-[140px] flex-1">
                        <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-last">
                            Last name
                        </label>
                        <input id="op-last" class="input w-full"
                               @bind="ViewModel.LastName" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-username">
                        Username
                    </label>
                    <input id="op-username" class="input w-full font-mono"
                           autocomplete="off"
                           @bind="ViewModel.Username" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-accent-700">
                        Unique here, not globally — a clinic may have a staff member with
                        the same username, because sign-in looks it up inside one clinic
                        code.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-email">
                        Email <span class="text-ink/45">(optional)</span>
                    </label>
                    <input id="op-email" class="input w-full" type="email"
                           @bind="ViewModel.Email" @bind:event="oninput" />
                </div>

                @if (ViewModel.ShowsPasswordField)
                {
                    <div>
                        <label class="mb-1 block text-xs text-ink/70" for="op-password">
                            Password
                        </label>

                        @*
                            Only on a new operator. An existing one's password is changed
                            through Set password, so it is an action somebody chose rather
                            than a field they could fill in by accident while correcting a
                            surname.

                            This comment is outside the tag on purpose — a Razor comment
                            inside an element's attribute list compiles without complaint
                            and silently drops the element and everything after it.
                        *@
                        <div class="flex gap-2">
                            <input id="op-password" class="input w-full"
                                   type="@(ViewModel.ShowPassword ? "text" : "password")"
                                   autocomplete="new-password"
                                   @bind="ViewModel.DraftPassword" @bind:event="oninput" />

                            <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary whitespace-nowrap"
                                    @onclick="() => ViewModel.ToggleShowPasswordCommand.Execute()">
                                @(ViewModel.ShowPassword ? "Hide" : "Show")
                            </button>
                        </div>

                        <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-accent-700">
                            Twelve characters or more — longer than a clinic's would need to
                            be, because this account can suspend every practice on the
                            platform.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                        <strong>Password</strong>
                        <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                            Changed through <strong>Set password</strong> on their row, not
                            from here.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }

                <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                    <strong>Role</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                        Super admin, and not a choice. The vendor's tenant holds no
                        patients, no diary and no invoices, so an operator without the
                        platform role would have nothing to open.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-t border-divider pt-3">
                    <button class="btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap"
                            disabled="@ViewModel.IsBusy"
                            @onclick="() => ViewModel.SaveOperatorCommand.ExecuteAsync()">
                        @(ViewModel.EditingIsNew ? "Add operator" : "Save operator")
                    </button>

                    <span class="text-xs text-ink/55">
                        Written to the vendor's own audit log.
                    </span>
                </div>
            }
            else
            {
                <h5 class="m-0">How these accounts work</h5>

                <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                    <strong>They sign in like anyone else</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                        With the vendor's clinic code, a username and a password — the same
                        screen and the same password check a practice's staff use. There is
                        no separate back door, which is the point: one sign-in path is one
                        thing to get right.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                    <strong>A clinic can never see them</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">
                        These accounts live in the vendor's own tenant, so the same filter
                        that stops one practice reading another's records keeps them out of
                        every practice's Users screen. The role is not offered in a clinic's
                        role picker either.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-surface p-3 text-[13px]">
                    <strong>What is still missing</strong>
                    <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-accent-700">
                        No MFA, no SSO and no self-service password reset — for an account
                        that administers every clinic, all three are worth more here than
                        anywhere else in the app. Setting a password above is the only
                        recovery there is, and it needs another operator to do it.
                    </div>
                </div>

                @if (ViewModel.AwaitingPasswordCount > 0)
                {
                    <div class="border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800">
                        <strong>@ViewModel.AwaitingPasswordCount waiting on a password.</strong>
                        <div class="mt-1 text-xs">
                            The account exists and appears in the list, but cannot sign in
                            until somebody sets one.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
            }
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// One operator's row: marked while the panel is about them, faded when deactivated.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The marker exists because the panel is in the other column. Without it, clicking
    /// Edit on the third of five rows changes something on the far side of the screen with
    /// nothing left behind to say which row it belongs to.
    ///
    /// Whole literal strings, every branch — Tailwind scans source text, so a class
    /// assembled from a base plus a conditional suffix never reaches the stylesheet.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string RowCss(bool isActive, bool isOpen)
    {
        if (isOpen)
        {
            return "flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-b border-divider border-l-2 border-l-accent bg-accent-100 py-2.5 pl-2.5";
        }

        return isActive
            ? "flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5"
            : "flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-b border-divider py-2.5 opacity-70";
    }
}
